{:edb/db-before-t 1650 :edb/db-after-t 1651 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534963 50 #inst "2026-10-08T12:20:34.961Z" 13194139534963 true] [17592186166590 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/df8fd1746a9043aa52213a7ae5fd57b7f6c7438d02f5993c99bc8bd294309f07.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = (x+1)(x-3)$\n\n- $y'= (x+1)(x-2)(x-3)$\n\n- $y' = x+1$" 13194139534963 true] [17592186166590 1100 "Which of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/7b4d47224f7078b77cef9c2893fcbe41.png)\n\n1. $y' = (x+1)(x-3)$\n2. $y'= (x+1)(x-2)(x-3)$\n3. $y' = x+1$" 13194139534963 false] [17592186166590 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/180dafc23275d08fea5565296f3d72c92aee7ac2b4b68077b2c084d005a23456.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x = -1$ and $x = 3,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation $y'= (x+1)(x-3)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=3.$\n\n- The equation $y'=(x+1)(x-2)(x-3)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=3,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $x=2,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation $y' = x+1$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at both $x=-1$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534963 true] [17592186166590 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/e96f55679cd970ecfefdac330a9bf7e0.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x = -1$ and $x = 3$, as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation $y'= (x+1)(x-3)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=3$.\n- The equation $y'=(x+1)(x-2)(x-3)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=3$, but it also evaluates to zero when $x=2$, which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n- The equation $y' = x+1$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at both $x=-1$ and $x=3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534963 false] [17592186166591 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/3ba95acb3d7d009a482f79e1224c0628ff43b2aa149df0918768c74576cc4d9a.png)\n\n \n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n- $y' = -x(x+4)^2$\n\n- $y' = -x^2(x+2)$\n\n- $y' = x^2(x+4)$" 13194139534963 true] [17592186166591 1100 "The diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/c8002ac8b196f968030864b2cd40eb74.png)\n\n1. $y' = -x(x+4)^2$\n2. $y' = -x^2(x+2)$\n3. $y' = x^2(x+4)$" 13194139534963 false] [17592186166591 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/3fb7bf013703760852c86c2b16147b278fd1707931e980dbb693412832cdc5f1.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x = -4$ and $x = 0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-x(x+4)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}(x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-4$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x <  -4$ | $-4 <  x <  0$ | $x > 0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\nearrow$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\searrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\ny' = -x(x+4)^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y'= -x(x+4)^2.$" 13194139534963 true] [17592186166591 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/9901d4ef96057b2693867474777d9aff.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x = -4$ and $x = 0$. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.Of the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n$y^{′}|=-x(x + 4)^{2}; y^{′}|= x^{2}(x + 4)$\n- Second, the lines $x=-4$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\nRegion$x < -4$$-4 < x < 0$$x > 0$Slope$\\nearrow$$\\nearrow$$\\searrow$Sign$\\color{blue}+$$\\color{blue}+$$\\color{red}-$\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n$y' = -x(x+4)^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\ny'= -x(x+4)^2\n$$" 13194139534963 false] [17592186166592 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/49c8ba9f215b8ec05263c46eda9809728e918e94808023969c6b57d034470115.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{2}{(x+2)^2}$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{1}{x+2}$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{2x}{x+2}$" 13194139534963 true] [17592186166592 1100 "Which of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/6d66b381562050b8d132c484ac1fa429.png)\n\n1. $y' = \\dfrac{2}{(x+2)^2}$\n2. $y' = \\dfrac{1}{x+2}$\n3. $y' = \\dfrac{2x}{x+2}$" 13194139534963 false] [17592186166592 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/65bedbf95e886a45884a914cab0100bacca6dc849606b15fe3a74ba7a4dd7dda.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-2.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to\\pm\\infty.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $x>-2$ and negative for $x< -2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation $y' =  \\dfrac{2}{(x+2)^2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for all $x \\neq  -2.$\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{1}{x+2}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $x>-2$ and negative for $x< -2,$ and $y'\\to 0$ as $x\\to\\pm\\infty.$\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{2x}{x+2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x}{x-2} = \\dfrac{2}{\\left(1-\\dfrac2x\\right)}\\to 2 \\quad\\text{as} \\quad x\\to \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to\\pm\\infty.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534963 true] [17592186166592 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/210333f72c3deff33c57c265f4a7f39c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-2$.\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to\\pm\\infty$.\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $x>-2$ and negative for $x< -2$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{2}{(x+2)^2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for all $x \\neq -2$.\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{1}{x+2}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $x>-2$ and negative for $x< -2$, and $y'\\to 0$ as $x\\to\\pm\\infty$.\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{2x}{x+2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{2x}{x-2} = \\dfrac{2}{\\left(1-\\dfrac2x\\right)}\\to 2 \\quad\\text{as} \\quad x\\to \\pm\\infty$.\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to\\pm\\infty$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534963 false] [17592186166642 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/b2b67d39de238bceb50e0f21d65d99434b4a7f3be553a6c1c12876ecef3e5cf1.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' =x^2 - 4$\n\n- $y' = x^2 - 4x + 4$\n\n- $y' = 4 - x^2$" 13194139534963 true] [17592186166642 1100 "Which of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/57973b45a55a252ab3207ceb63d783b2.png)\n\n1. $y' =x^2 - 4$\n2. $y' = x^2 - 4x + 4$\n3. $y' = 4 - x^2$" 13194139534963 false] [17592186166642 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/b3768fbcd892e8484f0fabfca29bd6365cfc382bde2dd39c6ca8d278911747db.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x = -2$ and $x = 2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x <                  -2$ | $-2 <                  x <                  2$ | $x > 2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\nearrow$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| Slope | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny' =x^2 - 4 = (x+2)(x-2)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\ny' = x^2 - 4x + 4 = (x-2)^2\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=2,$ but not at $x=-2.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny' = 4 - x^2 = (2+x)(2-x)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $x=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) does not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x <                  -2$ | $-2 <                  x <                  2$ | $x > 2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\searrow$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\searrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534963 true] [17592186166642 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/f688b03d6348fc4ba801b5e382ce2865.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x = -2$ and $x = 2$. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:Region$x < -2$$-2 < x < 2$$x > 2$Slope$\\nearrow$$\\searrow$$\\nearrow$Slope$\\color{blue}+$$\\color{red}-$$\\color{blue}+$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation\n$y' =x^2 - 4 = (x+2)(x-2)$\ngives the required behavior.\n- The equation\n$y' = x^2 - 4x + 4 = (x-2)^2$\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=2$, but not at $x=-2$.\n- The equation\n$y' = 4 - x^2 = (2+x)(2-x)$\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $x=2$, but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) does not correspond to the given slope field.Region$x < -2$$-2 < x < 2$$x > 2$Slope$\\searrow$$\\nearrow$$\\searrow$Sign$\\color{red}-$$\\color{blue}+$$\\color{red}-$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534963 false] [17592186166752 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322947 13194139534963 true] [17592186166752 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322954 13194139534963 true] [17592186166752 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322961 13194139534963 true] [17592186166752 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322968 13194139534963 true] [17592186166752 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323017 13194139534963 true] [17592186166753 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322926 13194139534963 true] [17592186166753 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322933 13194139534963 true] [17592186166753 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322940 13194139534963 true] [17592186166754 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322975 13194139534963 true] [17592186166754 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323003 13194139534963 true] [17592186166754 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323010 13194139534963 true] [17592186166754 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323038 13194139534963 true] [17592186166789 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322982 13194139534963 true] [17592186166789 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322989 13194139534963 true] [17592186166789 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322996 13194139534963 true] [17592186166789 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323024 13194139534963 true] [17592186166789 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323031 13194139534963 true] [17592186167392 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534963 true] [17592186167392 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/76c15fa584f5a216d23ef10b10fbae14c09b84714e49bd8db0cc7954cab8c0f3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x-2$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$" 13194139534963 true] [17592186167392 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/q-96094.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = x - 2$\n2. $y^{′} = x(x - 2)^{2}$\n3. $y^{′} = x(x - 2)$" 13194139534963 false] [17592186167392 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/111e679939475cabbfe9a40779de3fa1bf4f2de732f943a3b0396a3ee9bf3519.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x-2$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=2$ only.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$ gives the required behavior.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534963 true] [17592186167393 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534963 true] [17592186167393 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/252d52b07c6c5e7a43c7816e3eb769e3f5abb561b84ef144ba0eeff022557260.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139534963 true] [17592186167393 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/q-96095.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139534963 false] [17592186167393 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/dfd308fd43e27e7adaab6d40411cf33e53f0186faf5719c1ccbe9d934f552cca.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(x-1)(x+2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(x-1)(x+2) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(x-1)(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can dismiss the equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-1)(x+2).$ Although the right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $x=1,$ it also evaluates to zero when $x=0,$ which is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $x=1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-2$ | $-2<x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-1)(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=(x-1)(x+2)^{2}.$" 13194139534963 true] [17592186167396 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534963 true] [17592186167396 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/680bc0001fb95783ee70fc88a7106997bcb3dba2ad85a708164878d0f610ecfb.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{5x}{x-2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-2}$" 13194139534963 true] [17592186167396 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/q-96098.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = x(x - 2)$\n2. $y^{′} = \\frac{5x}{x - 2}$\n3. $y^{′} = \\frac{1}{x - 2}$" 13194139534963 false] [17592186167396 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/74b482b5b72421c9ee208034dc4727605e42874015d080130ffd7d18437af0da.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=2.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not approach $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to 2.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{5x}{x-2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x}{x-2}=\\frac{5}{(1-\\frac{2}{x})}\\to 5\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-2}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x-2}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322926 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322927 13194139534963 true] [17592186322926 1089 "q-115672" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322926 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534963 true] [17592186322926 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/a9c84c00dfdd0f84e8bf81dc4be8808794685a884f0e6bebd908da461e3de9a4.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322926 1113 #uuid "433ce550-1d93-55af-8282-9fdcc1fd2d1e" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322926 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/d7f1fccf2bb73f160faa04c058db6892f78f105e374516c97558f81121fc49f8.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}(2-x) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the three remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}(2-x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(2-x).$" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322927 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322928 13194139534963 true] [17592186322927 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322929 13194139534963 true] [17592186322927 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322930 13194139534963 true] [17592186322927 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322931 13194139534963 true] [17592186322927 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322932 13194139534963 true] [17592186322927 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534963 true] [17592186322927 1041 "selection" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322927 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322930 13194139534963 true] [17592186322927 1110 #uuid "05e0262f-9154-5c98-8c69-c1a7929da18d" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322928 1034 "{y}^{′}=-x(x+2)" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322928 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186322928 1111 #uuid "e44065c8-00f8-5235-a6b5-09994d41088f" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322929 1034 "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x+2)" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322929 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186322929 1111 #uuid "472fd94e-0014-55ac-9a8b-c617eaa96bda" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322930 1034 "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}(2-x)" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322930 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186322930 1111 #uuid "8d1a4c48-ade2-5642-a8ed-186b875534e5" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322931 1034 "{y}^{′}=x(x-2)^{2}" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322931 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186322931 1111 #uuid "2d42075f-5a58-58e3-9473-65b74c1a6a04" 13