22940 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/b16e7007b3a266291fa22c1760e55e5b5e0a3e9a2d4ea97303f379a1f372cd32.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322940 1113 #uuid "ba1cbc26-b6c5-5882-ab7e-b2a130fa1c84" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322940 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/d84c6743bd4cc1ab3306951f507f4691ea3c3f2075ead5f04328f64254d5a786.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(x+3) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(x+1)(x+3) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can dismiss the equation ${y}^{′}=x(x+1)(x+3).$ Although the right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $x=0,$ it also evaluates to zero when $x=-1,$ which is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x(x+3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=x(x+3).$" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322941 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322942 13194139534963 true] [17592186322941 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322943 13194139534963 true] [17592