eld is zero along the lines $x=-1$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x-3=(x+1)(x-3)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2(x+1)(x-3).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-1$ and $x=3$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-1$ | $-1<x<3$ | $x>3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x-3.$" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322997 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322998 13194139534963 true] [17592186322997 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322999 13194139534963 true] [17592186322997 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323000 13194139534963 true] [17592186322997 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323001 13194139534963 true] [17592186322997 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323002 13194139534963 true] [17592186322997 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534963 true] [17592186322997 1041 "selection" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322997 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323002 13194139534963 true] [17592186322997 1110 #uuid "bc9b8b92-ddf4-59db-bdef-6d67b3d66f05" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322998 1034 "{y}^{′}=-2(x+1)(x-3)" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322998 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186322998 1111 #uuid "396f0be6-bfcf-524e-9cf1-0c6de878d6c8" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322999 1034 "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-1" 13194139534963 true] [17592186322999 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186322999 1111 #uuid "8603513e-628c-5ca0-9a7b-1bc5412c1f52" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323000 1034 "{y}^{′}={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+2x" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323000 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186323000 1111 #uuid "1120938d-6049-5580-8087-d3676b62af4c" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323001 1034 "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-9" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323001 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186323001 1111 #uuid "285ba4b8-054d-58bc-ae0f-a1af572214d9" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323002 1034 "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x-3" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323002 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186323002 1111 #uuid "73697fb2-bac9-50a7-9e1d-7aea9a677ccb" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323003 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323004 13194139534963 true] [17592186323003 1089 "q-115852" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323003 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534963 true] [17592186323003 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/3e065edec23179a946ea28aa6f2b4f9a439ba23c014c38dded0cc7c756867a90.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." 13194139534963 true] [17592186323003 1113 #uuid "01d0f4b2-f8b0-5360-b72e-8c82d7b84058" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323003 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/5b92aa0ecaf7dd906a39c44e31a12e725802d9c7c5cdc046351030ae04f6809e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the solution curves have vertical asymptotes $x=-1$ and $x=2.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}=\\frac{1}{(x+1)(x-2)}\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x+1)(x-2)}.\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$ \n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}$ become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}$ do *not* become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323004 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323005 13194139534963 true] [17592186323004 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323006 13194139534963 true] [17592186323004 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323007 13194139534963 true] [17592186323004 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323008 13194139534963 true] [17592186323004 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323009 13194139534963 true] [17592186323004 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534963 true] [17592186323004 1041 "selection" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323004 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323006 13194139534963 true] [17592186323004 1110 #uuid "8edbf045-8881-5ee6-964a-2bc6a14571c5" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323005 1034 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323005 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186323005 1111 #uuid "d39ed27f-f059-5339-96a4-1f552c1bc2bf" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323006 1034 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323006 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186323006 1111 #uuid "3067ad22-375a-54b0-9c3c-075fa805b7c8" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323007 1034 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323007 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186323007 1111 #uuid "23fd4c7c-0a0b-5c35-9c75-a34232758c82" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323008 1034 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2}" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323008 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186323008 1111 #uuid "58bd2e7f-03fa-5f9b-9c84-fce215644331" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323009 1034 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-2}" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323009 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186323009 1111 #uuid "e16e4487-2323-5f42-92bc-bb55c718ed55" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323010 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323011 13194139534963 true] [17592186323010 1089 "q-115853" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323010 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534963 true] [17592186323010 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/a355d9b35a7ebf791a56116b0ee5b127d0dfd90d6958b5bebd04aa9ad09a9ddc.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." 13194139534963 true] [17592186323010 1113 #uuid "83a90d4b-eea7-5c39-bfee-4621ae6098ab" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323010 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/6293d40f222d4d81236808f7ab0a35dfa82e1187b3a4d24bbc841a1b158f1039.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the solution curves have vertical asymptotes $x=-2$ and $x=1.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-x-{x}^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{(x-1)(x+2)}\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+x-2}=\\frac{x}{(x-1)(x+2)}.\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $x=1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-2$ | $-2<x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-x-{x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323011 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323012 13194139534963 true] [17592186323011 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323013 13194139534963 true] [17592186323011 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323014 13194139534963 true] [17592186323011 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323015 13194139534963 true] [17592186323011 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323016 13194139534963 true] [17592186323011 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534963 true] [17592186323011 1041 "selection" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323011 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323016 13194139534963 true] [17592186323011 1110 #uuid "879098bc-c59a-5faa-a7c6-6b621608f1d0" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323012 1034 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+2}" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323012 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186323012 1111 #uuid "c2cb4fca-c312-5576-b40d-8fa135e643d2" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323013 1034 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{x+2}{1-{x}^{2}}" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323013 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186323013 1111 #uuid "fa2f1ebf-6891-51b0-aa0b-a3c21aedff2a" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323014 1034 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+x-2}" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323014 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186323014 1111 #uuid "64c490e6-f11c-5038-b740-a6fb059e230d" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323015 1034 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323015 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186323015 1111 #uuid "5853df92-ba4d-5cc4-bb29-007c2bb556e7" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323016 1034 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-x-{x}^{2}}" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323016 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186323016 1111 #uuid "46a367b7-d1a4-5457-a05f-cad7d94548be" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323017 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323018 13194139534963 true] [17592186323017 1089 "q-120025" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323017 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534963 true] [17592186323017 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/17cd72acbe8dd412d37c51ff992278d9664f6c5b1141a8604278fda76e57700f.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x-2$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x-2)$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x+1)(x-2)$" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323017 1113 #uuid "de6d895f-4494-5fa4-acc4-ab90880d6dfc" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323017 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/5d6165488c8e41c16cf31b1b000a924344399c2b94a8fe5e7138c55adf97602b.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x-2$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at both $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x-2)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x+1)(x-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $x=-1,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323018 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323019 13194139534963 true] [17592186323018 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323020 13194139534963 true] [17592186323018 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323021 13194139534963 true] [17592186323018 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323022 13194139534963 true] [17592186323018 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323023 13194139534963 true] [17592186323018 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534963 true] [17592186323018 1041 "selection" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323018 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323023 13194139534963 true] [17592186323018 1110 #uuid "50576a3a-1a37-5b7b-b0a3-87ea7930c347" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323019 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323019 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534963 true] [17592186323019 1111 #uuid "31a15e01-646c-5afe-97d5-879308474de7" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323020 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323020 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534963 true] [17592186323020 1111 #uuid "c8cd421e-f528-53bd-a774-e26e26836eef" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323021 1034 "I only" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323021 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534963 true] [17592186323021 1111 #uuid "7ce63104-3a46-595c-9977-f8a9f853ca6b" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323022 1034 "III only" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323022 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534963 true] [17592186323022 1111 #uuid "2551f46a-36d1-5d90-874e-758698581d60" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323023 1034 "II only" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323023 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534963 true] [17592186323023 1111 #uuid "fc8b6ce1-975a-581a-b235-d7113b966cc0" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323024 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323025 13194139534963 true] [17592186323024 1089 "q-120070" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323024 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534963 true] [17592186323024 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/51273365c4a282dc3dedd9d7cd5ac7d805e1526aaeee1915e8bd95d8223c26d3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+3x$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}$" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323024 1113 #uuid "2ce3677b-f81c-5f71-ba2d-0905f9bffa60" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323024 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/a14651ffc883a8c24ad17bdd031658c3d4ae21db2cfded213f2ba911fdb9a765.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+3x=x(x+3)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x=x(x+1)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=0.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(x+3)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $x=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323025 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323026 13194139534963 true] [17592186323025 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323027 13194139534963 true] [17592186323025 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323028 13194139534963 true] [17592186323025 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323029 13194139534963 true] [17592186323025 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323030 13194139534963 true] [17592186323025 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534963 true] [17592186323025 1041 "selection" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323025 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323027 13194139534963 true] [17592186323025 1110 #uuid "59254fd9-0cc0-54a3-871b-0afc57beb24c" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323026 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323026 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534963 true] [17592186323026 1111 #uuid "b9eff459-3c71-5895-8fae-3d6beecb694a" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323027 1034 "I only" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323027 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534963 true] [17592186323027 1111 #uuid "634c4288-a0dd-56c1-ba08-07d3310ba18b" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323028 1034 "III only" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323028 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534963 true] [17592186323028 1111 #uuid "da55894b-e0db-5544-a761-bf9776533701" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323029 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323029 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534963 true] [17592186323029 1111 #uuid "70be9107-e562-5579-9575-baaa6d128076" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323030 1034 "II only" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323030 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534963 true] [17592186323030 1111 #uuid "e6d3acca-9253-5619-947f-f12026672040" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323031 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323032 13194139534963 true] [17592186323031 1089 "q-120072" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323031 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534963 true] [17592186323031 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/6e57e25ea43f23e9d157b3fa1c621749d3d1a88bab389af66449c915d9e03606.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323031 1113 #uuid "e93b7591-d0af-5d87-a1e4-0c5a1ef93f64" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323031 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/523f18dc7b081ecf6dcfa1f09c6fe715012fffba3e4e07d1dfd44c3d53cf702c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x=-1$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(x+1),\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x=x(x+1).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-1$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-1$ | $-1<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(x+1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}.$" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323033 13194139534963 true] [17592186323032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323034 13194139534963 true] [17592186323032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323035 13194139534963 true] [17592186323032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323036 13194139534963 true] [17592186323032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323037 13194139534963 true] [17592186323032 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534963 true] [17592186323032 1041 "selection" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323032 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323037 13194139534963 true] [17592186323032 1110 #uuid "be10418b-4747-5ff1-95e8-cd17aa91b105" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323033 1034 "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323033 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186323033 1111 #uuid "5bb14e93-6a33-55bd-a50b-17a08910e90d" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323034 1034 "{y}^{′}=1-{x}^{2}" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323034 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186323034 1111 #uuid "faf5dbc3-e436-5002-9d0f-a514258f2604" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323035 1034 "{y}^{′}={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323035 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186323035 1111 #uuid "9648aff0-ddf2-518f-abed-bafe568d4c62" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323036 1034 "{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+1" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323036 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186323036 1111 #uuid "88b26a1c-9ca1-564d-92f8-0b73c99cfab4" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323037 1034 "{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323037 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534963 true] [17592186323037 1111 #uuid "422f484f-ee93-55e8-9ec1-484f460bcadf" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323038 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323039 13194139534963 true] [17592186323038 1089 "q-120076" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323038 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534963 true] [17592186323038 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/5fdb2ddd3d07537c1e3d0d22d435928f7cd11dc7a71c67f1bf45292c82a61ef7.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x+1)$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x+1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323038 1113 #uuid "875466a4-b8ac-5bb9-91c1-86f9987a0d97" 13194139534963 true] [17592186323038 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/cf2ba44d8cc21ade2204a3784af7d09b492843339a8b98c4cde741b30dfa54db.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x+1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not approach $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to -1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x+1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not approach $\\i